<Base title="About">
  <header class="masthead">
    <p class="kicker">फल · about</p>
    <h1>What this is</h1>
    <p class="sub">A reading engine, not a chart calculator.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="chapter">
    <div class="body">
      <p>
        Birth data goes in. The reading comes out: ten chapters on the sectors of
        life, each written from conditions that Phaladīpikā states and this engine
        tests against the chart. The chart itself is never drawn — that is what a
        calculation platform is for.
      </p>
      <p>
        Every statement carries its verse. Open one and you see the Devanagari and
        the chapter-and-verse it came from. Where a citation could not be checked
        against a printed edition, it says so rather than implying authority it
        does not have.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two rules govern the harsh readings. Phaladīpikā 15.3 names a whole
        configuration, not a single clause; 15.6 states that two or three
        conditions must agree before a severe loss is declared. So a grave reading
        that rests on one placement is withheld, and the engine says that it is
        withholding it. No death date is produced at all: the classical arithmetic
        is disputed by the tradition itself, and a figure from a disputed method
        would be worse than none.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
